# tools/ales_reverb_width_witness.rish — reverb_width: reverberate the master WET, then set how wide the reverberated result sits, closing the width axis the reverb series opened (ALES212).
#
# Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES212.
# Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-122004_fill-ales212-lotus-reverb-width.md
# Stands on: lotus/stereo_reverb_preset.rye (ALES191 — the named-room stereo wet, run over the master unchanged, its StereoReverbPresetError forwarded
# whole), lotus/stereo_width.rye (ALES211 — the width control, encode/scale-the-side/decode, run over the wet result), lotus/mid_side.rye (ALES210 —
# the reversible mid/side primitive width spends), lotus/pan.rye (ALES10 — the StereoClip and its balance invariant), lotus/clock.rye (ALES5 — the
# validated sample clock). A keeper wants a reverb WIDE across the field for air, or NARROW toward the centre so the wash does not smear the mix —
# and that is exactly "reverberate, then widen".
#
# lotus/reverb_width.rye composes two proven stages with NO new audio arithmetic: validate the width fraction FIRST (a bad fraction refuses BadWidth
# before the reverb writes, so the master is left dry), reverberate wet in place (ALES191's stereo_reverb_preset, unchanged), widen the wet result
# (ALES211's stereo_width, the width already proven legal so it cannot fault). THE MONO COLLAPSE: width == 0 zeroes the wet's side, so both channels
# become the reverberated master's shared mid = (wl+wr)/2, a fully centred wash. THE WIDEN LAW: width == 2·den widens the wet — reverb_width IS
# reverberate-then-widen, so its result equals stereo_width applied to a fresh copy of the pure reverb byte for byte, and the per-sample |L−R| grows.
# THE UNITY EDGE: width == den is the mid/side round-trip over the reverberated master, within ≤ 1 LSB of the pure stereo reverb on both channels
# (ALES210's honest halved-side cost). THE CENTRED-INVARIANT LAW: a centred master reverberates to a centred wet whose side is zero, so width leaves
# it untouched at any setting — a mono source has no reverb width to turn. THE FAULT LAW: a bad width refuses BadWidth, a span past the clip
# DurationTooLong, an out-of-range span BadRange, each leaving the master DRY and untouched.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ales_reverb_width_witness.rish

say "ales-reverb-width: Language EN — reverb_width reverberates a stereo master WET, then sets how wide the reverberated result sits by scaling the wet's SIDE and recombining. It closes the width axis the long reverb series opened, with the primitive ALES210 laid and ALES211 first spent. A keeper wants a reverb wide across the whole field for air, or narrow toward the centre so the wash does not smear the mix — and that is exactly 'reverberate, then widen'. This proves the two proven stages compose whole: reverb_width invents nothing, it orders."
say "ales-reverb-width: Style Radiant — the shape is stated positively as a composition of two proven stages with no new audio arithmetic: validate the width fraction first (a zero or over-bound width refuses BadWidth before the reverb ever writes, so a bad width leaves the master fully dry), reverberate the master wet in place through ALES191's stereo_reverb_preset unchanged, then widen the now-wet master through ALES211's stereo_width, whose width was already proven legal so it cannot fault. The edges name the knob honestly. width == den is the mid/side round-trip over the reverberated master — within one LSB of the pure stereo reverb (ALES210's honest halved-side cost). width == 0 is the mono collapse: the wet's side is zeroed, so both channels become the reverberated master's shared mid, a fully centred wash. width past den widens: the reverberated channel difference grows, the wash pushed outward. A centred master reverberates through one shared bank to a centred wet, whose side is zero, so width leaves it untouched at any setting."
say "ales-reverb-width: Lens TAME — one bounded i16 StereoClip transformed in place; the balance invariant is asserted on entry and exit, the clock is asserted valid, and the width fraction is bounded (max_width_den 4096, max_width_num 32768, reused from ALES211) and checked BEFORE the reverb writes, so a bad width refuses BadWidth with the master dry (the atomicity crux — the precheck precedes the first write); the reverb then prechecks its own DurationTooLong / BadRange before writing, and stereo_width's only fault is the width it was already handed legal, so a legal call refuses whole up front or completes whole. It reuses ALES191's stereo_reverb_preset and ALES211's stereo_width over their public APIs, inventing no new primitive; the mono collapse is proven both channels identical and each the wet's mid, the widen law proven byte for byte against stereo_width over a fresh copy of the pure reverb with |L−R| grown per sample, the unity edge proven within ≤ 1 LSB of the pure reverb, the centred-invariant law proven identical channel-to-channel at the collapse and 2× settings; siloed to lotus/, no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no device, no speaker."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "lotus/bin/reverb_width"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p lotus/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build lotus/reverb_width.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "ales-reverb-width: build failed — ${build.err}"

let outfile = "lotus/bin/reverb_width.out"
let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest > ${outfile} 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "ales-reverb-width: selftest exited non-zero — see ${outfile}"

let shown = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${outfile}"]
say "ales-reverb-width: ${shown.out}"

let green = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -q 'GREEN ales-reverb-width' ${outfile} && echo yes"]
assert (trim green.out) == "yes" else "ales-reverb-width: selftest did not print the GREEN ales-reverb-width line"
